<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pi-Ramesses</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Per-Ramesses, Piramesse</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pi-Ramesses was the capital built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a>, at modern Qantir, on the site of the older city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaris">Avaris</a>. It held palaces, temples, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> workshops and barracks for campaigns into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. When the branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> beside it silted up around the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, it was abandoned, and many of its statues and blocks were carried to the new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanis">Tanis</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pi-ramesses.jpg" alt="Pi-Ramesses" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Iri-en-achti · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kolossalstatue_Qantir.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name means House of Ramesses. The city stood on the banks of the Pelusiac branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, the easternmost branch of the Delta in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a>. It had served as a summer palace under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seti-i">Seti I</a> and may have been founded by Ramesses I. Ramesses II was born and raised in the area, but geopolitical reasons may have mattered more: the city lay much closer to the Egyptian vassal states in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and to the border with the hostile Hittite empire, and the main corps of the army was encamped there, ready to meet incursions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> or Shasu nomads.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>With a population of over 300,000, Pi-Ramesses was one of the largest cities of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and it flourished for more than a century after Ramesses' death. Ground-penetrating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radar">radar</a> shows a huge central temple, a precinct of mansions on the river set in a grid of streets, and an irregular collection of houses and workshops in the east. Manfred Bietak's team found many canals and lakes and called it the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> of Egypt.</p><p>The Pelusiac branch began to silt up around 1060 BCE, leaving the city without water as the river settled in a new course to the west. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> of the Twenty-first Dynasty established <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanis">Tanis</a> on the new branch, 100 km to the north-west, as the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a>. They carried the Ramesside temples, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisks</a>, statues and sphinxes there, moving obelisks and statues weighing over 200 tons in one piece and dismantling major buildings to reassemble them. The old city became largely a quarry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Flinders Petrie dug at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanis">Tanis</a> in 1884 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-montet">Pierre Montet</a> explored it in the 1930s, and the masses of Ramesside stonework there led archaeologists to identify Tanis as Pi-Ramesses. It later became clear that none of these monuments originated at the site. In the 1960s Bietak mapped the branches of the ancient Delta and showed that the Pelusiac was the easternmost in Ramesses' reign, and excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaris">Tell el-Dab'a</a> and Qantir followed. Qantir was recognised as the site of Pi-Ramesses, about 30 km south of Tanis. The name is generally linked with the Raamses of Exodus 1:11, one of the cities the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> were forced to build.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pi-Ramesses” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The capital moved to Tanis in the north-eastern Delta, built largely from monuments carried from the abandoned Ramesside capital <b>Pi-Ramesses</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/twenty-first-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-first Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
